# Timber - ten woodwinds. Registers and tuning are done; the timbres are not.
#
# **Every patch says the note it is meant for, and that was most of what was
# wrong with it.** There were no `note=` lines at all, so the harness played
# a bassoon and a piccolo-register oboe at the same middle C - and a woodwind
# does not transpose. Each patch has a `body`, which is the tube it is built
# on, and playing a long way off it gets you the wrong partial or no partial:
# measured, the bassoon (body 58.3 Hz) makes no pitch above note 60 and the
# flute (261.6 Hz) makes none below 48, both of which are correct and are
# what a real one does.
#
# So the registers were measured rather than guessed - a sweep of every patch
# from note 30 to 78, looking for where it actually produces a pitch - and
# each `note=` is the middle of what came back. In their own registers they
# read, before and after:
#
#   Bass Clarinet  -15c -> +1c      Alto Sax      -7c -> +1c
#   Stopped Wood    -8c -> +2c      Oboe          -5c -> -0c
#   Bassoon         -5c -> +3c      Flute         -5c -> -2c
#
# Across the two octaves the audition phrase now plays, everything is inside
# ten cents and most of it inside three.
#
# **Forked was not playing a note at all.** `answer` was 0.4675 of a maximum
# 0.55, which is past the point where a fork fingering stops being a colour
# and becomes a note the tube cannot hold - it measured -4137 cents, which is
# no pitch. At 0.32 it plays, and it is still veiled, which is the point.
#
# **Levelled to -23 dB RMS**, spread 3.3 dB. Init is the only one outside it
# and has to be: an Init sets nothing by rule.
#
# **What is still wrong, and it is the part that needs an ear.** These do not
# yet sound like the instruments on the labels. The register is right and the
# tuning is right and the balance is right, and none of that is timbre - the
# reed, the embouchure, the lattice and the hole depth are all still as they
# were seeded. That is the next pass and it cannot be done from a table.
#
# Three measured holes, all inside a patch's own range rather than at its
# edge, so they are faults and not limits: the alto sax jumps a partial at
# eight semitones below its centre (+346 cents), the oboe makes no pitch an
# octave above its centre, and the clarinet reads -9c at its own note while
# reading +2 and +1 an octave either side.
#
# And the jets still speak at a speed that depends on pitch - the flute runs
# 350 ms at note 40 against 50 at note 70 - because the onset lift the reeds
# got drives a jet's gain solve far too hard and detunes it by nineteen
# cents. See Pipe::lift.
#
# The `edge` column is high, 5x to 12x, and it is the breath: measured,
# Stopped Wood reads the same with `keys` at 0.45 and at zero, and 7.2x with
# `breath` at zero. Broadband noise against a harmonic tone will always
# measure an onset brighter than its body, which is what a woodwind is.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Timber` lists
# them. A patch names only what it changes.

machine Timber
# A melody rather than one held note: one note is what one note sounds
# like, and a bank can pass that patch by patch and still be the same
# sound all the way down.
role    tune

patch Init

patch Clarinet  note=52
  attack         0.02 s
  breath         0.1
  keys           0.2

  volume         1.05
patch "Bass Clarinet"  note=42
  body           73.4 Hz
  breath         0.16
  embouchure     0.5
  keys           0.35
  lattice        1000 Hz
  pressure       0.9
  tongue         0.6

  volume         1.12
patch "Alto Sax"  note=45
  attack         0.015 s
  body           138.6 Hz
  bore           #1
  breath         0.2
  embouchure     0.5
  keys           0.3
  lattice        900 Hz
  pressure       0.95
  tongue         0.75
  vibrato        9 cents

  volume         1.03
patch Oboe  note=54
  attack         0.012 s
  body           233.1 Hz
  bore           #1
  breath         0.08
  embouchure     0.7
  family         #1
  keys           0.2
  lattice        1600 Hz
  pressure       0.95
  tongue         0.8
  vibrato        12 cents

  volume         1.31
patch Bassoon  note=45
  attack         0.025 s
  body           58.3 Hz
  bore           #1
  embouchure     0.6
  family         #1
  keys           0.4
  lattice        700 Hz
  pressure       0.9

  volume         1.24
patch Flute  note=63
  attack         0.04 s
  body           261.6 Hz
  bore           #1
  breath         0.45
  family         #2
  keys           0.15
  lattice        1400 Hz
  pressure       0.8
  tongue         0.35
  vibrato        10.8 cents

  volume         0.44
patch "Pan Pipe"  note=60
  aim            0.1
  attack         0.06 s
  body           196 Hz
  breath         0.6
  family         #2
  jet            0.55
  lattice        2200 Hz
  pressure       0.7
  tongue         0.2

  volume         0.71
patch "Stopped Wood"  note=42
  answer         0.385
  attack         0.05 s
  body           110 Hz
  breath         0.3
  drive          0.2
  embouchure     0.45
  holes          0.9
  lattice        420 Hz
  pressure       1
  tongue         0.5

  volume         0.38
patch Forked  note=48
  # 0.4675, seeded from what shipped, is past the point where the fork
  # stops being a colour and becomes a note the tube cannot hold: it read
  # -4137 cents, which is no pitch at all. It plays at 0.32.
  answer         0.32
  attack         0.04 s
  below          1.74
  bore           #1
  breath         0.25
  embouchure     0.5
  family         #1
  fingering      0.8
  flutter        0.35
  lattice        1200 Hz
  pressure       1
  tongue         0.6

  volume         0.77